CAT = "s6Funds"; SEG = "FND"
Q = [
('An interval fund lets shareholders sell shares back to the fund:',
 'At set periodic intervals', ['On any day the NYSE is open', 'Only on an exchange', 'Never before the fund ends'],
 'An interval fund is a closed-end fund that offers to repurchase a set percentage of shares at stated intervals, such as quarterly. It is not redeemable daily.'),
('Unlike a retail money market fund, an institutional prime money market fund:',
 'Has a floating NAV', ['Keeps a stable $1 NAV', 'Is FDIC insured', 'Sells only to retirees'],
 'Under SEC Rule 2a-7, institutional prime and institutional tax-exempt money market funds price at a floating NAV. Retail and government funds may keep a stable $1 price.'),
('A government money market fund must keep at least what share of assets in cash, government securities or related repos?',
 '99.5%', ['80%', '90%', '95%'],
 'Rule 2a-7 defines a government money market fund as one investing 99.5% or more of total assets in cash, government securities and repurchase agreements collateralized by them.'),
('A fund named "Midwest Utilities Fund" must normally invest at least what share of assets as its name suggests?',
 '80%', ['50%', '65%', '90%'],
 'Rule 35d-1, the names rule, requires a fund whose name suggests a focus to adopt a policy of investing at least 80% of assets in line with that focus.'),
('Under Rule 22c-1, a purchase order a fund receives after that day\'s NAV is struck is priced at:',
 'The next day\'s NAV', ['That day\'s NAV', 'The prior day\'s NAV', 'An average of the two'],
 'Forward pricing means every order gets the NAV next computed after the fund or its agent receives it, so a late order waits for the next calculation.'),
('Accepting a fund order after the 4 p.m. close at that day\'s NAV is:',
 'Late trading', ['Market timing', 'Forward pricing', 'Breakpoint selling'],
 'Late trading gives the buyer a price already set before later news was known. It violates Rule 22c-1\'s forward pricing requirement.'),
('Rapid trading in and out of a fund to exploit stale prices is called:',
 'Market timing', ['Late trading', 'Dollar-cost averaging', 'Breakpoint selling'],
 'Market timing is not always illegal, but it dilutes long-term holders, and funds disclose policies to restrict it. Helping a customer evade those policies is a violation.'),
('Under Section 22(e), a fund may suspend redemptions longer than seven days when:',
 'NYSE trading is restricted', ['The fund lost money', 'The manager is on leave', 'Redemptions top purchases'],
 'Section 22(e) allows suspension only when the NYSE is closed other than for normal holidays or trading is restricted, in an SEC-defined emergency, or by SEC order.'),
('A fund\'s NAV is $9.40 and its sales charge is 6% of the offering price. The public offering price is:',
 '$10.00', ['$8.84', '$9.96', '$10.34'],
 'Sales charges are a percentage of the offering price: POP = NAV / (1 - 0.06) = $9.40 / 0.94 = $10.00. Adding 6% to NAV gives $9.96, the wrong base.'),
('Under FINRA Rule 2341, the most a fund with no asset-based charge may impose in front-end and deferred sales charges is:',
 '8.5% of offering price', ['5.75% of offering price', '3% of the net asset value', '10% of net asset value'],
 'Rule 2341 caps total sales charges at 8.5% of the offering price for funds without an asset-based charge, and lower if the fund lacks breakpoints or rights of accumulation.'),
('Rule 2341 caps a fund\'s asset-based sales charge at what yearly percentage of average net assets?',
 '0.75%', ['0.25%', '1.00%', '1.50%'],
 'The asset-based sales charge, the distribution part of a 12b-1 fee, may not exceed 0.75% a year. A separate service fee may add up to 0.25%.'),
('Under Rule 12b-1, the fund\'s board must review written reports of plan spending at least:',
 'Quarterly', ['Monthly', 'Annually', 'Every two years'],
 'Rule 12b-1 requires the people directing plan payments to give the board a written report of amounts spent and why at least quarterly.'),
('A 12b-1 plan stays in effect only if the board approves its continuation at least:',
 'Annually', ['Every five years', 'At each NAV calculation', 'Only at launch'],
 'Rule 12b-1 requires the plan to be renewed at least annually by the board, including a majority of independent directors.'),
('An investor holds fund shares worth $43,000 and invests $9,000 more. With rights of accumulation and a $50,000 breakpoint, the new purchase:',
 'Gets the breakpoint rate', ['Pays the full load', 'Gets half the discount', 'Needs a letter of intent'],
 'Rights of accumulation count the value of shares already held: $43,000 + $9,000 = $52,000, which crosses the $50,000 breakpoint for the new purchase.'),
('An investor signs a letter of intent but never reaches the breakpoint. The fund will typically:',
 'Charge the higher sales load', ['Cancel all purchases', 'Return all the escrowed shares', 'Waive the shortfall'],
 'Funds hold some shares in escrow under a letter of intent. If the amount is not reached, the prospectus typically has enough escrowed shares redeemed to pay the sales charge actually owed.'),
('Fund families generally let which purchases be combined to reach a breakpoint?',
 'A spouse\'s and minor children\'s', ['Members of an investment club', 'Neighbors in one town', 'All customers of the same rep'],
 'Each prospectus defines who may combine purchases (FINRA\'s breakpoint guidance tells investors to check it), usually the investor, spouse and minor children, and certain trusts. Unrelated groups cannot pool for a discount.'),
('A Class B share\'s contingent deferred sales charge usually:',
 'Falls each year shares are held', ['Rises with the share price', 'Is charged on each purchase', 'Applies only to dividends'],
 'The CDSC on back-end load shares typically steps down over several years, often to zero, and the shares then convert to a lower-fee class.'),
('Class B shares usually convert after several years into:',
 'Class A shares', ['Class C shares', 'Money market shares', 'Closed-end shares'],
 'Once the CDSC period ends, Class B shares commonly convert to Class A shares, which carry lower ongoing 12b-1 fees.'),
('Class C shares typically carry:',
 'Level 12b-1 fees, no front load', ['A front load but no 12b-1 fee at all', 'A load that falls to zero', 'No fees of any kind'],
 'Class C shares are level-load shares: no front-end charge, a higher yearly 12b-1 fee that does not end, and often a small CDSC in the first year.'),
('Under Rule 2341, reinvested dividends in most funds are bought:',
 'Without a sales charge', ['At the full offering price', 'At half the sales charge', 'Only at year end'],
 'Rule 2341 bars a sales charge on shares bought by reinvesting dividends, except for funds whose registration became effective before April 2000.'),
('Under a fixed-dollar systematic withdrawal plan, the number of shares redeemed each time:',
 'Varies with the NAV', ['Stays the same each time', 'Always rises over time', 'Is set by the fund board'],
 'Taking the same dollar amount means selling more shares when NAV is low and fewer when it is high.'),
('A fixed-dollar withdrawal plan can use up the principal when:',
 'Withdrawals exceed fund returns', ['The NAV rises steadily each year', 'Dividends are reinvested', 'The fund lowers its fees'],
 'If the amount taken each period is more than the fund earns, the plan sells shares from principal and the account may run out. Withdrawal plans are never guaranteed.'),
('Shares of a new closed-end fund are first sold to the public:',
 'In an IPO at a fixed price', ['By daily redemption at NAV', 'At NAV with no spread', 'Only through rights'],
 'A closed-end fund raises its capital once, through an underwritten IPO at a set price. After that the shares trade in the secondary market.'),
('Under Section 18, an open-end fund may not issue:',
 'Senior securities like bonds', ['More than one share class', 'Shares to non-U.S. investors', 'Redeemable shares'],
 'Section 18(f) bars open-end funds from issuing senior securities, except bank borrowing with 300% asset coverage. Multiple share classes are permitted by rule.'),
('Under Section 13(a), a fund needs shareholder approval to:',
 'Switch to non-diversified', ['Pay a quarterly dividend', 'Hire a new transfer agent', 'Calculate its daily NAV'],
 'Section 13(a) requires a majority shareholder vote to change subclassification, such as diversified to non-diversified, or to deviate from fundamental policies.'),
('Under Section 16(a), a fund\'s directors are elected by:',
 'Its shareholders', ['Its investment adviser', 'The SEC', 'Its principal underwriter'],
 'Section 16(a) requires directors to be elected by shareholders, with limited exceptions for filling vacancies between meetings.'),
('Within the 75% basket of a diversified fund, it may own no more than what share of one issuer\'s voting securities?',
 '10%', ['5%', '25%', '50%'],
 'Section 5(b)(1) defines a diversified fund: in 75% of its assets, no more than 5% of total assets in one issuer and no more than 10% of that issuer\'s voting securities.'),
('Under Rule 19a-1, a fund paying a dividend from a source other than net income must:',
 'State the source in writing', ['Get SEC approval before paying', 'Delay it one quarter', 'Pay it only in new fund shares'],
 'Rule 19a-1 requires a written statement with the payment showing the source, such as capital gains or return of capital, so holders do not mistake it for income.'),
('Rule 19b-1 generally limits a regulated investment company to how many capital gain distributions per tax year?',
 'One', ['Two', 'Four', 'Twelve'],
 'Rule 19b-1 allows one capital gain distribution a year, plus a small supplemental one of up to 10% to meet tax rules.'),
('ETF shares are created and redeemed in large blocks by:',
 'Authorized participants', ['Individual retail investors', 'The fund\'s board', 'Index providers'],
 'Authorized participants exchange baskets of securities for creation units with the ETF, which keeps the market price close to NAV.'),
('A unit investment trust has no:',
 'Board of directors', ['Sponsoring firm', 'Trustee bank', 'Portfolio of securities'],
 'A UIT is organized under a trust indenture with a sponsor and trustee but no board of directors and no investment adviser.'),
('A balanced fund invests in:',
 'Both stocks and bonds', ['Only blue-chip stocks', 'Only Treasury bills', 'One industry\'s stocks'],
 'A balanced fund holds a mix of stocks and bonds, and sometimes cash, to combine growth, income and lower volatility.'),
('A target-date fund\'s asset mix typically becomes:',
 'More conservative over time', ['More aggressive as it ages', 'All stocks at the target', 'Fixed for its whole life'],
 'Life-cycle funds follow a glide path, shifting from stocks toward bonds and cash as the target year approaches.'),
('Compared with a broad index fund, a sector fund has more:',
 'Concentration risk', ['Diversification', 'Inflation protection', 'Credit risk protection'],
 'A sector fund holds companies in one industry, so problems in that industry hit most of the portfolio at once.'),
('Under Section 10(a), at least what share of a fund\'s board must be independent?',
 '40%', ['25%', '50%', '75%'],
 'Section 10(a) bars a board on which more than 60% of members are interested persons, so at least 40% must be independent.'),
('Under Section 22(d), a dealer selling fund shares to the public must use:',
 'Prospectus offering price', ['A price it negotiates freely', 'The market bid price', 'The prior day\'s closing price'],
 'Section 22(d) requires sales at the current public offering price in the prospectus, so dealers cannot discount loads below the stated schedule.'),
]
